      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "We collected the data of 288 patients with malignant skin tumours. We analysed the postoperative pain assessed by a visual analogue scale (VAS) to evaluate the quality of our standard peri-operative pain therapy after a radical inguinal and iliacal lymph node dissection (RILND) as well as the influence of postoperative surgical complications on the level of pain."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHOD",
          "content": "The postoperative level of pain of 85 patients with malignant skin tumours who underwent a RILND between August 2003 and December 2007 was recorded prospectively. Patients received a standardised perioperative pain therapy according to level I or II of the World Health Organisation (WHO) ladder of pain. The efficiency of our pain therapy was registered via VAS in the morning of the first three postoperative days."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Using our standard pain therapy, we determined a VAS < 30 in rest during the first three postoperative days, but significantly more pain (VAS median 50-30) (p < 0.001) under stress. Patients with surgical complications in the postoperative period (n = 71) had significantly more pain in the postoperative period compared to patients with a regular postoperative course (p = 0.047)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Immediately after a RILND, an analgesic therapy according to level I or II of the WHO pain ladder does not seem to be effective enough. Postoperative surgical complications lead to a higher VAS level of pain in the postoperative period."
        }
      ]},
